SILICONFOREST LICENSING NOTICE

SiliconForest contains code under several licenses. Original SiliconForest
code is MIT-licensed. Third-party code retains its existing terms. Some
files still need provenance review.

This file is a notice. It is not a grant of rights in the repository, and
it does not change the licence of any file. The repository is not licensed
as a whole under the MIT License.


Original SiliconForest code
---------------------------

The MIT grant, together with its copyright notice, is
LICENSE-SILICONFOREST-MIT:

  Copyright (c) 2026 Chris Kearney and the SiliconForest contributors

That grant covers original SiliconForest code only: files written for
SiliconForest that were not ported, adopted, or copied from another
project. LICENSES/MIT.txt is a generic template and still contains the
placeholders "<year>" and "<copyright holders>". It is not the
SiliconForest grant. Use LICENSE-SILICONFOREST-MIT.


Third-party code
----------------

Apple's APSL-2.0 code, GPL code, and the other third-party code in this
tree keep the licence of the code they derive from. Full texts that were
copied into the repository are in LICENSES/. LICENSES/README.md says where
each copy came from. NOTICE is the provenance record. A directory name, a
NOTICE file, or an SPDX line on a neighbouring file does not by itself
state the licence of the file in front of you. Where a file does not
identify its source, this notice does not decide it.

Crediting a project in README.md ("Credits and lineage") or in NOTICE does
not satisfy that project's licence. GPL-2.0 and APSL-2.0 code, and the
other licences in this tree, carry their own terms: the licence text,
copyright notices, source-availability conditions, and the APSL's
modification notices. Files from different sources sit in the same
directories, so the credits list does not say which terms apply to which
file.

Present as texts in LICENSES/, and named by NOTICE or by LICENSES/README.md
as used by some code here:

  MIT                  LICENSES/MIT.txt (template; the named grant is
                       LICENSE-SILICONFOREST-MIT)
  APSL-2.0             LICENSES/APSL-2.0.txt
  GPL-2.0-only         LICENSES/GPL-2.0-only.txt
                       (also the text of GPL-2.0-or-later, as that README notes)
  GPL-3.0-only         LICENSES/GPL-3.0-only.txt
  LGPL-2.1-or-later    LICENSES/LGPL-2.1-or-later.txt
  Apache-2.0           LICENSES/Apache-2.0.txt
  MPL-2.0              LICENSES/MPL-2.0.txt
  BSD-2-Clause         LICENSES/BSD-2-Clause.txt
  ISC                  LICENSES/ISC.txt
  OFL-1.1              LICENSES/OFL-1.1.txt
  Linux-syscall-note   LICENSES/Linux-syscall-note.txt
  0BSD                 LICENSES/third-party/Indium-LICENSE-0BSD.txt

LICENSES/third-party/ also holds licence files copied with their copyright
lines: PureDarwin's own PUREDARWIN_LICENSE.txt (BSD-2-Clause), Finch (MIT
and Apache-2.0), and m1n1 (MIT). PUREDARWIN_LICENSE.txt is the PureDarwin
Project's licence file. It is not the licence of every file in PureDarwin.

The PureDarwin base (NOTICE: branch upstream-xnu-12377 at c713d8fb) is
itself mixed. A PureDarwin maintainer wrote that it includes pure MPL-2.0,
MPL-2.0 OR MIT, and some tri-licensed files, as well as BSD-2-Clause:

  https://github.com/chriskearney/siliconforest/issues/23#issuecomment-6984090313

The tri-licence expressions were not named in that comment and are not
assigned to files here. NOTICE also records some newer PureDarwin files as
MPL-2.0 OR BSD-2-Clause from their own notices, and Apple's XNU and other
Darwin code as APSL-2.0 or the other licence that file's header names.
The licence of a PureDarwin file is the notice on that file in upstream
PureDarwin:

  https://github.com/PureDarwin/PureDarwin

Code taken from Linux and Asahi Linux is recorded as GPL-2.0-only, or as a
dual licence where the file says so. The vendored Chonkstep copy's LICENSE
and Cargo.toml (license = "GPL-3.0-only") are GPL-3.0-only; NOTICE also
records MIT licence files inside that copy. Finch is recorded as MIT OR
Apache-2.0. OpenBSD bwfm is recorded as ISC. m1n1 is recorded as MIT.
These are the statements in NOTICE and in the licence files.

Files that still need provenance review, missing directory NOTICE files,
SPDX tags without a copyright line, and the open questions about
distributing a kernel collection or other combined binary are recorded in
docs/RELEASE_CHECKLIST.md (items 4 and 6, and the notes added there).
Nothing in this notice states that such a combination may be distributed.

How the texts and the provenance record fit together is in LICENSING.md.
The short status is in LICENSE_STATUS.md.
